WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:12.584
A quote needs two answers: the customer from the CRM, which can take 2 seconds, and a credit limit check that fails after 100 ms.

2
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.129
Both run on a shared executor.

3
00:00:15.129 --> 00:00:19.555
At 100 ms the limit check fails, and so does the request.

4
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:21.949
What happens to the CRM call?

5
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:25.293
Nothing stops it.

6
00:00:25.293 --> 00:00:30.857
It runs for the full two seconds, holding a connection, and nobody reads its answer.

7
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:36.096
Now let the request own its subtasks, like a tree owns its branches.

8
00:00:36.096 --> 00:00:41.316
The scope forks each one on its own virtual thread, then joins: it waits for them.

9
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:43.920
At 100 ms the limit check fails.

10
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.754
The scope cancels the sibling at once.

11
00:00:46.754 --> 00:00:52.348
It interrupts the CRM call, which is blocked reading a socket, so it stops.

12
00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:55.840
join throws that first failure at once.

13
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.191
At 100 ms, not at 2 seconds.

14
00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:01.605
Then close waits until every branch has really ended.

15
00:01:01.605 --> 00:01:05.112
When the block exits, nothing it started is still running.

16
00:01:05.333 --> 00:01:07.305
That is structured concurrency.

17
00:01:07.305 --> 00:01:13.731
A subtask can't outlive the block that started it, the way a local variable can't outlive its method.

18
00:01:15.533 --> 00:01:17.848
Context can flow down the same tree.

19
00:01:17.848 --> 00:01:20.677
Bind a scoped value, the tenant, at the top.

20
00:01:20.677 --> 00:01:22.991
Each subtask reads that one binding.

21
00:01:22.991 --> 00:01:26.141
Nobody can change it, and it ends with the block.

22
00:01:26.367 --> 00:01:29.662
Check the JDK before you copy code.

23
00:01:29.662 --> 00:01:38.607
StructuredTaskScope is a preview API in JDK 25, 26 and 27, so it needs the enable preview flag.

24
00:01:38.607 --> 00:01:41.149
It becomes final in JDK 28.

25
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:45.516
Scoped values are already final, since JDK 25.

26
00:01:45.516 --> 00:01:47.140
They need no flag.

27
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:50.516
Here it is in JDK 25 code.

28
00:01:50.516 --> 00:01:52.965
Kotlin calls the same API.

29
00:01:53.767 --> 00:01:54.383
The rule.

30
00:01:54.383 --> 00:01:57.876
Every subtask ends before the block that forked it.

31
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.337
Check yourself.

32
00:01:59.337 --> 00:02:01.646
One subtask fails at 100 ms.

33
00:02:01.646 --> 00:02:04.203
The other would take 2 seconds.

34
00:02:04.203 --> 00:02:05.935
When does join throw?

35
00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.001
At about 100 ms.

36
00:02:10.001 --> 00:02:13.672
The first failure cancels the other subtask.
